{
  "requirement": "The SDK offers this `Sources` method and exposes the `outline_source`\ncommand through its source tools.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Sources {\n    // ...\n\n    /// Outline one file snapshot.\n    pub async fn parse(\n        &self,\n        path: impl AsRef<Path>,\n    ) -> Result<OutlineContent, SourceError>;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Structural check.** The requirement has two obligations. The first is a `Sources` method `pub async fn parse(&self, path: impl AsRef<Path>) -> Result<OutlineContent, SourceError>`. The second is that the SDK exposes the `outline_source` command through its source tools.\n\n**Method signature.**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[parse]` has the required signature. It is `pub async`, takes `&self` and `path: impl AsRef<Path>`, and returns `Result<crate::outline::OutlineContent, SourceError>`.\n- `OutlineContent` is the pub struct in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/contents.rs#struct[OutlineContent]`. `SourceError` is the enum in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#enum[SourceError]`.\n- The body reads the file through `self.read` and outlines it with `OutlineContent::from_source`. A parse error becomes `SourceError::Invalid`. This matches \"Outline one file snapshot\".\n\n**Tool exposure.**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs#impl[Session<'_, D>/ToolSession<D>]/fn[dispatch]` handles `\"outline_source\"` by calling `self.tools.sources.parse(string(\"path\")?)`. This delegates directly to `Sources::parse`.\n- Its `fn[definitions]` declares the `outline_source` tool.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/sources.rs#impl[SourceSession<...>/ToolSession<D>]/fn[definitions]` also declares `outline_source`. It takes a `resource` argument, and its dispatch arm for `\"outline_source\" | \"read_source\"` goes through the provider-based analysis rather than `Sources::parse`.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#impl[SourceAnalysis<P>]/fn[parse]` is a `#[cfg(test)]` helper that calls the provider's parse. It is a related root rather than a deviation from the required `Sources::parse` signature.\n\n**Result.** No structural gap. There are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments and none are needed. The subject only requires the method and the tool exposure, so I have not assessed behavior or tests here. Those belong to the refining `outline_source.behavior` and `outline_source.inputs` requirements.",
